ally amazing  thing about this book though because to  jump to the end a little bit  I liked this quite a bit for as thick as  this is it actually if you were to to  sketch out everything that happens  chronologically it's not actually all  that much this it's not long because of  huge epic scope it's long because of the  way  Pratchett writes and the amount of  detail and the flourish that he gives to  his stories he reminds me a bit of  Douglas Adams and I want to but in a  very specific way is not it's not quite  the same style of humor and obviously  atoms usually messed about with sci-fi  but what I mean is what makes the this  story good is less the nuts and bolts of  the actual plot but just the use of  language in describing it though the  turns of phrase that he will come up  with in order to describe something  though or the way something felt for the  characters that is the kind of stuff  that make that it kind of ends up  patting out what would otherwise be a  fairly straightforward story but it's  because he has a flourish and a flair  and describing things but it not like  purple prose but just like dry-weight in  in the way that things get described and  it just it just makes it really really  fun alright plot for this I'm I don't  think I'll end up getting to spoilers  but if any we're gonna go there I'll let  you know so this is set in Discworld in  a small country called Boer gravia which  is at war and has been for a very very  long time  the main character is Polly who is a  young woman  who cuts her hair short stuffs some  socks down her trousers and pretends to  be a man to join the army in order to  find her brother who she believes to be  captured by the enemy that's your  starting premise so right off the bat  your initial start is like oh okay so  this is gonna be a fairly typical boy  becomes a man's story through war  that's pretty tried and true a ground to  go with but the twist is it's a girl  pretending to be a boy okay cool and  that's where it starts but what I really  enjoy about this is that the story kept  going in directions I expected but then  doing things like once it goes starts  going down that path it would do things  differently from how I expected them and  that's kind of a hard way to describe  but I'll talk about some of the  characters as a way just to describe  that so two of the the major supporting  characters are the the sergeant and the  lieutenant that Polly is answering to  the sergeant is sergeant Jack Rowe who  is a very rut you know rough-and-tumble  on the ground I'll take care of my boys  you know kind of sergeant it's it's a  bit of a military trope and the  lieutenant is a bit of a oh well you  know I I just I've read books and I  understand numbers and I I'm sure we'll  give them a jolly good wallop you know  kind of thing you know he's foppish he's  he's not battle-hardened he has no  experience again fairly tried-and-true  trope now normally in this situation  what you would expect is for the  lieutenant to be completely useless and  nothing but a detriment and the end the  sergeant to be the one who who knows  what's actually going on in and you know  it has all the right answers and that  happens to an extent but it also gets  undercut because as it goes along you  realize sometimes Jack rums way of going  about the  no that's actually not a great idea and  in fact the lieutenant has a better idea  but also not always so like what you  realize about say lieutenant blouse is  that yeah his name is blouse is that he  doesn't have he definitely has none of  Jack Rome's experience but he's still  looking at things with more intelligence  and more analytical nature than people  on the battlefield tend to and he's he  is coming up intermittently with things  that nobody else on the squad considers  and is actually a good idea and works  really well and there are times that he  stands his ground and pulls his rank  he's like oh crap he's actually right  but then that